(e) proxies (closed forms validated in (a)); all chains give the same values, so these are exact for every f
   order-free proxy: w_cyc = 1/2; brooms: wB_j = (1/4)/(j(j+1)) for every j, plus (1/4)/(i(i+1)) more at j = 2^i
   (the second family imitates K(k) being small for simple k such as powers of two); no partial chains.
   For each k the loss is about the cheaper of two routes: cyclic law, log2(N-k) + log2(1/w_cyc) above
   log2(N-1)_{k-1}; broom B_k, log2(1/wB_k) above it.  Thm 2.3(1): min{K(k), log2(N-k)} + c.
